---

[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is a realistic and clean Gaussian Splatting&nbsp; model. It is free of messy floating artifacts,&nbsp;&nbsp; and the clarity remains impressive even&nbsp; when zooming in close. In today’s video,&nbsp;&nbsp; we will cover every step, including data&nbsp; acquisition, Gaussian Splatting generation,&nbsp;&nbsp; editing, and rendering. The data used to generate&nbsp; the Gaussian model in this video was captured&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using a five-year-old iPhone. Most importantly,&nbsp; all the software we are using here is free.&nbsp;&nbsp; We use the Blackmagic Camera App for data&nbsp; collection, MipMap Desktop for generating the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Gaussian Splatting model, and SuperSplat&nbsp; for editing and generating the rendered&nbsp;&nbsp; video. I have placed links to all the software&nbsp; in the description below. You do not need to&nbsp;&nbsp; spend a penny to replicate the workflow in&nbsp; this tutorial. I will explain every step in&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as much detail as possible. We are not just&nbsp; developing professional software, we are also&nbsp;&nbsp; committed to consistently producing low-barrier,&nbsp; high-quality instructional videos. If you like&nbsp;&nbsp; this kind of content, please click the Like&nbsp; button below and subscribe to our channel to&nbsp;&nbsp; be the first to receive our latest content. Let’s get started. First, open the Blackmagic&nbsp;&nbsp; Camera App. We will enter the settings interface&nbsp; and reset all settings to configure the software&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from scratch. Switch to the Record panel under&nbsp; the Settings tab. Select Codec. H264 encoding has&nbsp;&nbsp; the best compatibility, meaning you can view the&nbsp; final video normally on most devices and software,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but it limits the maximum frame rate in 4K&nbsp; mode to 30 frames per second. H265 encoding&nbsp;&nbsp; is more efficient, allowing for 60 frames per&nbsp; second in 4K mode. With the same image quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the resulting video file size is smaller,&nbsp; which saves storage space on your phone. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp; we select the more efficient H265 encoding. Set&nbsp; the bitrate to the maximum. Bitrate affects video&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quality. While some minute differences may not be&nbsp; distinguishable to the human eye, they can have an&nbsp;&nbsp; impact on stereo vision algorithms. Confirm that&nbsp; the Resolution is set to 4K. Enable Timelapse&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Recording. This is a very useful setting. The&nbsp; data we use for Gaussian reconstruction does&nbsp;&nbsp; not require every single frame of the video. This&nbsp; setting allows us to reduce phone heating during&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recording and significantly reduces the video&nbsp; file size. For example, if I set it to save one&nbsp;&nbsp; frame every 10 frames, the video file will be&nbsp; nearly 10 times smaller. You might be curious,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'why do we use the 60 frames per second mode?&nbsp; Because the 60 fps readout mode reduces the&nbsp;&nbsp; imaging distortion caused by rolling shutter,&nbsp; making it match better with the mathematical model&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'established by the reconstruction software for&nbsp; the camera, thereby resulting in a better model. Next, switch to the Camera panel and turn off Lens&nbsp; Correction. Reconstruction software mathematically&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'models the camera\'s lens distortion. The built-in&nbsp; distortion correction function of the camera often&nbsp;&nbsp; destroys the match between the imaging process&nbsp; and the mathematical model of distortion modeling,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so make sure to turn it off. Lock the camera&nbsp; to shoot horizontally. Switch to the Audio&nbsp;&nbsp; panel. We do not need the audio recorded&nbsp; during shooting, so you can turn it off.&nbsp; Mastering the exposure triangle is the first step&nbsp; to great photography, but it is even more critical&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if you are doing 3D reconstruction. Let\'s break&nbsp; down how Aperture, Shutter Speed, and ISO affect&nbsp;&nbsp; your results. First, Aperture controls your depth&nbsp; of field. A large aperture, like f/1.4, lets in&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more light but blurs the background, whereas a&nbsp; small aperture, like f/32, keeps the entire scene&nbsp;&nbsp; sharp. When using an Phone for 3D reconstruction,&nbsp; the approach changes slightly because you cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mechanically adjust the aperture. However, you&nbsp; must be aware that the different lenses on your&nbsp;&nbsp; phone have different natural depths of field.&nbsp; Second, Shutter Speed determines motion blur. Slow&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'speeds can cause moving objects to look blurry,&nbsp; while fast speeds freeze action instantly. Third,&nbsp;&nbsp; ISO controls the sensor\'s sensitivity.&nbsp; A high ISO allows you to shoot in the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dark but adds grainy noise to the image. Now let’s switch to the recorder view.&nbsp;&nbsp; Click the stabilization icon at the bottom&nbsp; and turn off camera stabilization. Digital&nbsp;&nbsp; stabilization of the camera is achieved by&nbsp; panning, zooming, and rotating the image.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'These operations change the camera\'s intrinsic&nbsp; parameters, specifically the focal length&nbsp;&nbsp; and principal point. Keeping camera parameters&nbsp; constant during the entire shooting process makes&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'subsequent reconstruction more stable and avoids&nbsp; overfitting during camera parameter calculation,&nbsp;&nbsp; which can affect model quality. Of course,&nbsp; turning off stabilization will bring about&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'issues with shooting jitter and blur. If you&nbsp; have a handheld gimbal for your phone, using&nbsp;&nbsp; the gimbal\'s physical stabilization will greatly&nbsp; alleviate this problem. Later in the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I will also mention how to avoid having blurred&nbsp; video frames participate in the reconstruction. Phone cameras typically have three lenses, and for&nbsp; reconstruction tasks, each has its pros and cons.&nbsp;&nbsp; The ultra-wide lens is a fixed-focus lens, which&nbsp; means its shooting range is almost always clear&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and will not be blurred or out of focus. More&nbsp; importantly, it does not need to focus, ensuring&nbsp;&nbsp; that the camera\'s intrinsic parameters are&nbsp; consistent throughout the entire shooting process,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or even after restarting the camera. The manual&nbsp; focus settings in the software have no effect&nbsp;&nbsp; on it at all, which is an excellent trait for&nbsp; reconstruction tasks. However, its lens distortion&nbsp;&nbsp; is more complex, and the image quality is not as&nbsp; good as the other lenses, especially when ambient&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'light is insufficient. The wide-angle lens has the&nbsp; best image quality, but cameras with this lens are&nbsp;&nbsp; equipped with sensor-shift stabilization. It seems&nbsp; this stabilization cannot be completely turned off&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through software settings, which may bring some&nbsp; changes to the camera\'s intrinsic parameters.&nbsp;&nbsp; Also, as you can see with nearby objects, the&nbsp; subject can easily become blurred and out of focus&nbsp;&nbsp; as the focus distance changes. The telephoto lens&nbsp; also has this problem. We expect to keep camera&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'parameters constant during the shooting process,&nbsp; so we need to fix a focus distance. Then, keep a&nbsp;&nbsp; close distance to shoot the object, avoiding large&nbsp; variations in shooting distance. To set the focus&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'distance, you can first switch to auto-focus and&nbsp; then cancel auto-focus to lock the corresponding&nbsp;&nbsp; focus distance. If you need to shoot at different&nbsp; distances, you can collect data in multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'batches, using different focus distances for each,&nbsp; and then group the cameras during reconstruction&nbsp;&nbsp; settings. To prevent motion blur during shooting,&nbsp; we set a faster shutter speed and keep it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'constant. It is recommended that the shutter&nbsp; speed is not slower than one five-hundredth&nbsp;&nbsp; of a second. Click the ISO icon and set it to the&nbsp; minimum value to get better image quality. If this&nbsp;&nbsp; setting makes the image too dark, you may need&nbsp; an environment with better lighting. Then switch&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the White Balance setting, adjust the color&nbsp; temperature until the color seen on the phone is&nbsp;&nbsp; closest to the color seen by your naked eye, and&nbsp; then lock the white balance. Once all the above&nbsp;&nbsp; settings are completed, you can largely ensure&nbsp; that the camera\'s intrinsic parameters remain&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'constant during the data collection process. Click&nbsp; the start recording button to begin recording. This is the video used in this&nbsp; tutorial. It has not been sped up;&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the video only records frames at intervals. When&nbsp; shooting, try to keep the camera at an oblique&nbsp;&nbsp; downward angle. Having too much background&nbsp; and sky enter the camera frame will make the&nbsp;&nbsp; final result messy and will also cause more&nbsp; computation to be spent on the background,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which we do not care about. When shooting,&nbsp; try to orbit from different angles,&nbsp;&nbsp; moving as slowly and smoothly as possible. You&nbsp; can orbit around a local part of the object;&nbsp;&nbsp; not every photo needs to contain the whole&nbsp; object, but ensure that all parts of the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'object are captured. There is another good method:&nbsp; imagine if the model were already generated, from&nbsp;&nbsp; which angles would you view this model? Then it&nbsp; is best to include similar angles when shooting.'

[08:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Data collection is complete. Let\'s open the MipMap&nbsp; software to generate the Gaussian Splatting model.&nbsp;&nbsp; If you encounter problems registering an&nbsp; account or using the software, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'check our previously recorded detailed tutorial.&nbsp; The link is in the description below the video. Click Import Video, select the recorded&nbsp; video file, and enter the video frame&nbsp;&nbsp; extraction interface. Set the corresponding&nbsp; extraction parameters. The free version of&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'MipMap supports reconstruction of up to 500&nbsp; photos per single task. After setting up,&nbsp;&nbsp; click Confirm. Wait for the extraction to finish.&nbsp; The extraction here is relatively simple and fast.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If your recorded video has many blur issues, you&nbsp; can use the sharp-frame web app to extract video&nbsp;&nbsp; frames. It will automatically select clear video&nbsp; frames, and you can set the interval and quantity&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of frame extraction, though it also requires more&nbsp; processing time. Back in the MipMap software,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'click the Camera button to enter the camera&nbsp; parameter setting interface. Setting the correct&nbsp;&nbsp; camera focal length parameter as an initial&nbsp; value can help with camera pose recovery. Click&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the calculator icon to perform focal length unit&nbsp; conversion. We used a telephoto lens for shooting,&nbsp;&nbsp; and its 35mm equivalent focal length is 65mm. We&nbsp; use the 35mm equivalent focal length to convert&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the pixel focal length. Click Confirm to complete&nbsp; the camera parameter settings. Select Ultra High&nbsp;&nbsp; model quality. Turn on the Gaussian Splatting&nbsp; result switch. Each type of result takes a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'certain amount of time to compute. If you do not&nbsp; need them, turn off the 3D mesh and point cloud&nbsp;&nbsp; results. Click Start Reconstruction. Just wait&nbsp; for the model generation. Before recording this&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tutorial, I had already generated this model, so&nbsp; let’s switch to the completed model task. In the&nbsp;&nbsp; camera pose interface, you can view the camera&nbsp; positions during shooting and the sparse point&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cloud of the scene. You can adjust the size of&nbsp; the camera icons. Click the GS button to switch to&nbsp;&nbsp; the Gaussian Splatting model. You can explore and&nbsp; browse the details of the model in this interface.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Click the View Product button to open the&nbsp; Gaussian Splatting result model. We will&nbsp;&nbsp; use the SuperSplat tool for editing to make the&nbsp; model cleaner. Click the File menu, Import Model,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and select the PLY result we generated.&nbsp; By default, you will see the point cloud&nbsp;&nbsp; of the Gaussian result. Click the button on&nbsp; the right to hide the Gaussian point cloud.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Sometimes when you drag to browse the model, it&nbsp; does not rotate as you expect. This affects the&nbsp;&nbsp; model browsing experience. At this point, you&nbsp; can adjust the model\'s coordinate system. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we first set the Z-axis rotation to zero. Rotate&nbsp; the model to align the coordinate system diagram&nbsp;&nbsp; in the upper right corner of the model. You can&nbsp; see that the model does not align well with the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coordinate axes and looks a bit tilted. It needs&nbsp; to be rotated by some angle along the X-axis. You&nbsp;&nbsp; do not need to know the specific angle; click in&nbsp; the input box for the X-axis on the left and drag&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'left or right until the model looks straight, then&nbsp; release the mouse. After adjusting the coordinate&nbsp;&nbsp; system, the rotation operation is much smoother.&nbsp; Left-click to rotate, right-click to drag,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and use the scroll wheel to zoom in and&nbsp; out. You can view the model from all angles. To edit the model, you first need to select&nbsp; the Gaussian spheres you want to edit. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let\'s look at its rich selection tools. These&nbsp; selection tools are easy to understand. The&nbsp;&nbsp; rectangular marquee tool selects, and the selected&nbsp; Gaussian ellipsoids will be highlighted in yellow.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can press the Delete key on the keyboard to&nbsp; remove the selected Gaussian ellipsoids. However,&nbsp;&nbsp; note that the geometry of Gaussian ellipsoids&nbsp; has two display states. In center mode,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the selection is penetrating, meaning all Gaussian&nbsp; ellipsoids within the selection range from this&nbsp;&nbsp; viewing angle will be selected. In ring mode,&nbsp; the selection operation will only select the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nearest Gaussian ellipsoids in the viewing&nbsp; direction and will not select the remaining&nbsp;&nbsp; occluded Gaussian ellipsoids. There&nbsp; are many other selection tools:&nbsp; For freehand shapes, use the Lasso Select. Just&nbsp; draw the outline you want directly on the screen.&nbsp;'

[13:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you need precise, straight lines, switch&nbsp; to Polygon Select. Click to place your points,&nbsp;&nbsp; and double-click to close the shape. For a more organic feel, try the Brush&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Select. This lets you \'paint\' your selection.&nbsp; A handy shortcut here is using the bracket keys&nbsp;&nbsp; on your keyboard to quickly resize the brush. To clean up messy noise or floating artifacts,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Flood Select is a lifesaver.&nbsp; Click anywhere to auto-select an area,&nbsp;&nbsp; and use the slider to adjust the sensitivity.&nbsp; It’s perfect for isolating those stray floaters.&nbsp;'

[14:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Finally, for 3D control, there is the Sphere&nbsp; Select. When you click the "Set" button,&nbsp;&nbsp; a sphere will appear.​ then adjust its size and&nbsp; position to select everything inside that volume.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Click Invert Selection in the selection menu to&nbsp; select all Gaussian ellipsoids outside the sphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and delete them. These selection tools are&nbsp; based on spatial position. In addition to these,&nbsp;&nbsp; there are selection tools based on histogram&nbsp; statistics. Open the Splat Data panel, select the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'required statistical metric from the dropdown, and&nbsp; after selecting the statistic, drag to select in&nbsp;&nbsp; the histogram area on the right. Some statistical&nbsp; metrics may be very concentrated in a very small&nbsp;&nbsp; range; in this case, turning on Log Scale can make&nbsp; the distribution more intuitive. With these tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can flexibly choose tools to edit and process&nbsp; the model. After the model cleaning is complete,&nbsp;&nbsp; you can set specified viewing angles as keyframes&nbsp; to constrain the trajectory and camera orientation&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the model showcase video. Once finished, you&nbsp; can choose to share the model publicly with the&nbsp;&nbsp; community, or export the edited model. You&nbsp; can also render it as a video for sharing,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just like the clip at the beginning of this&nbsp; video. After sharing the model to the community,&nbsp;&nbsp; the model will first be displayed following the&nbsp; trajectory you edited, but of course, you can&nbsp;&nbsp; also use the mouse and keyboard to interactively&nbsp; browse the model. I have placed the model link&nbsp;&nbsp; for this tutorial in the description below the&nbsp; video, so you can explore the model freely.'

[16:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, that covers everything for this video.&nbsp; Your likes and follows are our greatest support&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and our biggest motivation to produce and&nbsp; share tutorials. Thank you for watching.'
